From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Open Arc Authors <dev@openarc.invalid>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 18:09:39 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] openarc: chrome: let an embedding app own the Mac UI

Adds chrome::SetEmbedderOwnsUI() / EmbedderOwnsUI() (Mac only,
chrome/browser/embedder_owned_ui_mac.h). Open Arc runs Chrome's browser
process inside its own SwiftUI app: the app supplies NSApp (an
NSApplication subclass implementing CrAppControlProtocol), its delegate,
the main menu, every window and the main run loop, and creates profiles
and WebContents itself. Upstream //chrome assumes it owns all of these.

When the mode is on:
- RegisterBrowserCrApp() accepts the host's NSApp instead of creating
  BrowserCrApplication.
- ChromeBrowserMainPartsMac creates no AppController (NSApp's delegate)
  and no main menu, and does not swizzle the Services menu;
  +[AppController sharedController] returns nil.
- ChromeBrowserMainParts loads no initial profile and no last-opened
  profiles, runs no StartupBrowserCreator (so no first-run tabs, profile
  picker, session restore or startup browser) and sets no quit closure,
  so keep-alive changes never start Chrome's own shutdown; the embedder
  shuts the browser process down. A second launch with the same user
  data directory is acknowledged and dropped. The updater scheduler and
  the code-signature clone (an updater protection) are off.
- GetBrowserWindowCreationStatusForProfile() refuses every Browser, so
  code paths that would open a Chrome window fall back to doing nothing.
- BrowserProcessPlatformPart::AttemptExit() asks NSApp to terminate
  without AppController's quit confirmation.

Normal Chrome never calls SetEmbedderOwnsUI() and is unchanged. Needed
by plan task C6b (keep //chrome without its UI, decision Q7). There is
no upstream equivalent; Chrome has no supported embedding mode.
